tringent laws of nature are insurmountable; similarly, if a curse is uttered by a }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 br\'e4hma\'eba,}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  that curse is also insurmountable. But }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{
\cf1\insrsid11288628  also says that the curses or benedictions of the material world are, after all, material creations. The }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 Caitanya-carit\'e4m\'e5ta}{\cf1\insrsid11288628 
 also confirms that that which is accepted in this material world to be a benediction and that which is taken to be a curse are both on the same platform because they are material. To get out of this material contamination, one should take shel
ter of the Supreme Personality of Godhead, as recommended in }{\cs347\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{\cf1\insrsid11288628 : }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 m\'e4m eva ye prapadyante m\'e4y\'e4m et\'e4\'e0 taranti te.}{\cf1\insrsid11288628 
 The best path is to transcend all material curses and benedictions and take shelter of the Supreme Lord, K\'e5\'f1\'eba, and remain in a transcendental position. Persons who have taken shelter of K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a are always peaceful; they are never cursed by anyone, nor do they attempt to curse anyone. That is a transcendental position.
\par 
