iva's excellent character is described. In spite of the cursing and countercursing between the parties of Dak\'f1a and \'c7iva, because he is the greatest Vai\'f1\'ebava he was so sober that he did not say anything. A Vai\'f1\'eb
ava is always tolerant, and Lord \'c7iva is considered the topmost Vai\'f1\'ebava, so his character, as shown in this scene, is excellent. He became morose because he knew that these people, both his men and Dak\'f1
a's, were unnecessarily cursing and countercursing one another, without any interest in spiritual life. From his point of view, he did not see anyone as lower or higher, because he is a Vai\'f1\'ebava. As stated in }{\cs347\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 Bhagavad-
g\'e9t\'e4}{\cf1\insrsid11288628 , }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 pa\'eb\'f2it\'e4\'f9 sama-dar\'e7ina\'f9:}{\cf1\insrsid11288628 
 one who is perfectly learned does not see anyone as lesser or greater because he sees everyone from the spiritual platform. Thus the only alternative left to Lord \'c7iva was to leave in order to stop his follower, Nand\'e9\'e7vara, as well as Bh\'e5
gu Muni, from cursing and countercursing in that way.
\par 
