a, left the arena of sacrifice, the sacrifice was not stopped; the sages went on for many years in order to satisfy the Supreme Lord. The sacrifice was not destroyed for want of \'c7iva and Dak\'f1
a, and the sages went on with their activities. In other words, it may be assumed that if one does not worship the demigods, even up to Lord \'c7iva and Brahm\'e4, one can nevertheless satisfy the Supreme Personality of Godhead. This is also confirmed in 
}{\cs347\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{\cf1\insrsid11288628 . }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 K\'e4mais tais tair h\'e5ta j\'ef\'e4n\'e4\'f9 prapadyante 'nya-devat\'e4\'f9.}{\cf1\insrsid11288628 
 Persons who are impelled by lust and desire go to the demigods to derive some material benefit. }{\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  uses the very specific words }{\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 na\'f1\'f6i-buddhi\'f9}{
\cf1\insrsid11288628 , meaning persons who have lost their sense or intelligence. Only such persons care for demigods and want to derive material benefit from them. Of course, this does not mean that one should not show respect 
to the demigods, but there is no need to worship them. One who is honest may be faithful to the government, but he does not need to bribe the government servants. Bribery is illegal; one does not bribe a government servant, but that does not mean that he 
does not show him respect. Similarly, one who engages in the transcendental loving service of the Supreme Lord does not need to worship any demigod, nor does he have any tendency to show disrespect to the demigods. Elsewhere in }{
\cs347\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  it is stated, }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 ye 'py anya-devat\'e4-bhakt\'e4 yajante \'e7raddhay\'e4nvit\'e4\'f9.}{\cf1\insrsid11288628 
 The Lord says that anyone who worships the demigods is also worshiping Him, but he is worshiping }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 avidhi-p\'fcrvakam,}{\cf1\insrsid11288628 
 which means without following the regulative principles. The regulative principle is to wors
hip the Supreme Personality of Godhead. Worship of demigods may indirectly be worship of the Personality of Godhead, but it is not regulated. By worshiping the Supreme Lord, one automatically serves all the demigods because they are parts and parcels of t
he whole. If one supplies water to the root of a tree, all the parts of the tree, such as the leaves and branches, are automatically satisfied, and if one supplies food to the stomach, all the limbs of the body\emdash the hands, legs, fingers, etc.
\emdash are nourished. 
Thus by worshiping the Supreme Personality of Godhead one can satisfy all the demigods, but by worshiping all the demigods one does not completely worship the Supreme Lord. Therefore worship of the demigods is irregular, and it is disrespectful to the scr
iptural injunctions.
\par }\pard\plain \s216\qj \fi357\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11288628 \f35\fs28\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cf1\insrsid11288628 In this age of Kali it is practically impossible to perform the }{
\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 deva-yaj\'efa}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  or sacrifices to the demigods. As such, in this age }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 \'c7r\'e9mad-Bh\'e4gavatam}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  recommends }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 sa\'eck\'e9
rtana-yaj\'efa.}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 Yaj\'efai\'f9 sa\'eck\'e9rtana-pr\'e4yair yajanti hi sumedhasa\'f9}{\cf1\insrsid11288628 . "In this age the intelligent person completes the performances of all kinds of }{
