\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  it is said that a liberated person is always in mental equilibriu
m in both the distress and the happiness of this material world. Therefore the question may now be raised why a liberated personality like Lord \'c7iva was so unhappy because of the words of Dak\'f1a. The answer is given by \'c7r\'e9la Vi\'e7van\'e4
tha Cakravart\'e9 \'d6h\'e4kura. Lord \'c7iva is }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 \'e4tm\'e4r\'e4ma,}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  or situated in complete self-realization, but because he is the incarnation in charge of the material mode of ignorance, }{
\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 tamogu\'eba,}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  he is sometimes affected by the pleasure and pain of the material world. The difference between the ple
asure and pain of this material world and that of the spiritual world is that in the spiritual world the effect is qualitatively absolute. Therefore one may feel sorry in the absolute world, but the manifestation of so-called pain is always full of bliss.
 For instance, once Lord K\'e5\'f1\'eba, in His childhood, was chastised by His mother, Ya\'e7od\'e4, and Lord K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a cried. But although He shed tears from His eyes, this is not to be considered a reaction of the mode of ignorance, for the incident was full of transcendental pleasure. When K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a was playing in so many ways, sometimes it appeared that He caused distress to the }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 gop\'e9s,}{\cf1\insrsid11288628 
 but actually such dealings were full of transcendental bliss. That is the difference between the material and spiritual worlds. The spiritual 
world, where everything is pure, is pervertedly reflected in this material world. Since everything in the spiritual world is absolute, in the spiritual varieties of apparent pleasure and pain there is no other perception than eternal bliss, whereas in the
 material world, because everything is contaminated by the modes of material nature, there are feelings of pleasure and pain. Therefore because Lord \'c7
iva, although a fully self-realized person, was in charge of the material mode of ignorance, he felt sorrow.
\par 
