g up, welcoming one another and offering obeisances. But those who are elevated to the transcendental platform, being intelligent, offer such respects to the Supersoul, who is sitting within the body, not to the person identified with the body.
\par }\pard \s289\qc \fi360\li0\ri0\sb36\sa36\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11288628 {\fs28\cf11\insrsid11288628\charrsid3754542 PURPORT
\par }\pard\plain \s280\qj \li0\ri0\sb360\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11288628 \b\f35\fs28\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v\insrsid11288628 PURPORT
\par }\pard\plain \s124\qj \fi360\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11288628 \f35\fs28\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cf1\insrsid11288628 It may be argued that since Dak\'f1a was the father-in-law of Lord \'c7
iva, it was certainly the duty of Lord \'c7iva to offer him respect. In answer to that argument it is explained here that when a learned person stands up or offers obeisances in welcome, he
 offers respect to the Supersoul, who is sitting within everyone's heart. It is seen, therefore, among Vai\'f1\'eb
avas, that even when a disciple offers obeisances to his spiritual master, the spiritual master immediately returns the obeisances because they are 
mutually offered not to the body but to the Supersoul. Therefore the spiritual master also offers respect to the Supersoul situated in the body of the disciple. The Lord says in }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 \'c7r\'e9mad-Bh\'e4gavatam}{
\cf1\insrsid11288628  that offering respect to His devotee is more valuable than offering respect to Himself. Devotees do not identify with the body, so offering respect to a Vai\'f1\'ebava means offering respect to Vi\'f1\'eb
u. It is stated also that, as a matter of etiquette, as soon as one sees a Vai\'f1\'ebava he must immediately offer him respect, indicating the Supersoul sitting within. A Vai\'f1\'ebava sees the body as a temple of Vi\'f1\'ebu. Since Lord \'c7
iva had already offered respect to the Supersoul in K\'e5\'f1\'eba consciousness, offering respect to Dak\'f1a, who identified with his body, was already performed. Ther
e was no need to offer respect to his body because that is not directed by any Vedic injunction.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s304\qr \li0\ri0\sb300\sa120\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11288628 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid11288628 SB 4.3.23
