My dear father, the opulence which we possess is impossible either for you or for your flatterers to imagine because persons who engage in fruitive activities by performing great sacrifices are concerned with sat
isfying their bodily necessities by eating foodstuff offered as a sacrifice. We can exhibit our opulences simply by desiring to do so. This can be achieved only by great personalities who are renounced self-realized souls.
\par }\pard \s289\qc \fi360\li0\ri0\sb36\sa36\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11288628 {\fs28\cf11\insrsid11288628\charrsid3754542 PURPORT
\par }\pard\plain \s280\qj \li0\ri0\sb360\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11288628 \b\f35\fs28\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v\insrsid11288628 PURPORT
\par }\pard\plain \s124\qj \fi360\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11288628 \f35\fs28\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid11288628 Sat\'e9's father was
 under the impression that he was exalted in both prestige and opulence and that he had offered his daughter to a person who was not only poor but devoid of all culture. Her father might have been thinking that although she was a chaste woman, greatly adh
erent to her husband, her husband was in a deplorable condition. To counteract such thoughts, Sat\'e9 said that the opulence possessed by her husband could not be understood by materialistic persons like Dak\'f1
a and his followers, who were flatterers and were engaged in fruitive activities. Her husband's position was different. He possessed all opulences, but he did not like to exhibit them. Therefore such opulences are called }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 
avyakta,}{\insrsid11288628  or unmanifested. But if required, simply by willing, Lord \'c7iva can show his wonderful opulences, and such an event is predicted here, for it would soon occur. The opulence which Lord \'c7
iva possesses is enjoyable in renunciation and love of God, not in material exhibition of sense gratificatory methods. Such opulences are possessed by personalities like the Kum\'e4ras, N\'e4rada and Lord \'c7
iva, not by others. In this verse the performers of the Vedic rituals are condemned. They have been described here as }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 dh\'fcma-vartmabhi\'f9,}{\insrsid11288628 
 those who maintain themselves on the remnants of sacrificial foodstuff. There are two kinds of foodstuff offered in sacrifice. One kind is food offered in fruitive ritualistic sacrifices, and the other, the best, is food offered to Vi\'f1\'eb
u. Although in all cases Vi\'f1\'ebu is the chief Deity on the sacrificial altar, the performers of fruitive rituals aim to satisfy various demigods to achieve in return some material prosperity. Real sacrifice, however, is to satisfy Lord Vi\'f1\'eb
u, and the remnants of such sacrifices are beneficial for advancement in devotional service. The process of elevation by performing sacrifices other than those aimed at Vi\'f1\'ebu is very slow, and therefore it has been condemned in this verse. Vi\'e7van
\'e4tha Cakravart\'e9 has described the ritualistic performers to be like crows because crows delight in eating the remnants of food which has been thrown into the dustbin. All the }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 br\'e4hma\'ebas}{\insrsid11288628 
 who were present for the sacrifice were also condemned by Sat\'e9.}{\cf1\insrsid11288628\charrsid7623626 
\par }\pard\plain \s216\qj \fi357\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11288628 \f35\fs28\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cf1\insrsid11288628 Whether or not King Dak\'f1
a and his flatterers could understand the position of Lord \'c7iva, Sat\'e9 wanted to impress upon her father that he should not think her husband to be without opulence. Sat\'e9, being the devoted wife of Lord \'c7
iva, offers all kinds of material opulences to the worshipers of Lord \'c7iva. This fact is explained in the }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 \'c7r\'e9mad-Bh\'e4gavatam,}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  in the Tenth Canto. Lord \'c7iva
's worshipers sometimes appear to be more opulent than the worshipers of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu because Durg\'e4, or Sat\'e9, being the superintendent in charge of material affairs, can offer all material opulences to the worshipers of Lord \'c7
iva in order to glorify her husband, but the worshipers of Vi\'f1\'ebu are meant for spiritual elevation, and therefore their material opulence is sometimes found to decrease. These points are very nicely discussed in the Tenth Canto.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s304\qr \li0\ri0\sb300\sa120\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11288628 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid11288628 SB 4.4.22
