\par }\pard\plain \s280\qj \li0\ri0\sb360\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11288628 \b\f35\fs28\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v\insrsid11288628 TRANSLATION
\par }\pard\plain \s289\qj \fi360\li0\ri0\sb36\sa36\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11288628 \b\f35\fs24\cf13\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\insrsid11288628 
My dear lord, I am very impudent for not accepting your instructions, but this is not my fault. It is due to my being born in a k\'f1atriya family. My stepmother, Suruci, has pierced my heart with her hars
h words. Therefore your valuable instruction does not stand in my heart.
\par }\pard \s289\qc \fi360\li0\ri0\sb36\sa36\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11288628 {\fs28\cf11\insrsid11288628\charrsid3754542 PURPORT
\par }\pard\plain \s280\qj \li0\ri0\sb360\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11288628 \b\f35\fs28\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v\insrsid11288628 PURPORT
\par }\pard\plain \s124\qj \fi360\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11288628 \f35\fs28\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cf1\insrsid11288628 
It is said that the heart or mind is just like an earthen pot; once broken, it cannot be repaired by any means. Dhruva Mah\'e4r\'e4ja gave this example to N\'e4rada Muni. He sa
id that his heart, having been pierced by the arrows of his stepmother's harsh words, felt so broken that nothing seemed valuable but his desire to counteract her insult. His stepmother had said that because he was born from the womb of Sun\'e9
ti, a neglected queen of Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Utt\'e4nap\'e4da, Dhruva Mah\'e4r\'e4ja was not fit to sit either on the throne or on his father's lap. In other words, according to his stepmother, he could not be declared king. Dhruva Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja's determination was to become king of a planet exalted even beyond that possessed by Lord Brahm\'e4, the greatest of all the demigods.
\par }\pard\plain \s216\qj \fi357\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11288628 \f35\fs28\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cf1\insrsid11288628 Dhruva Mah\'e4r\'e4ja indirectly informed the great sage N\'e4
rada that there are four kinds of human spirit\emdash the brahminical spirit, the }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 k\'f1atriya}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  spirit, the }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 vai\'e7ya}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  spirit and the }{
\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 \'e7\'fcdra}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  spirit. The spirit of one caste is not applicable to the members of another. The philosophical spirit enunciated by N\'e4rada Muni might have been suitable for a }{
\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 br\'e4hma\'eba}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  spirit, but it was not suitable for a }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 k\'f1atriya.}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  Dhruva frankly admitted that he was lack
ing in brahminical humility and was therefore unable to accept the philosophy of N\'e4rada Muni.
\par The statements of Dhruva Mah\'e4r\'e4ja indicate that unless a child is trained according to his tendency, there is no possibility of his developing his particular spir
it. It was the duty of the spiritual master or teacher to observe the psychological movement of a particular boy and thus train him in a particular occupational duty. Dhruva Mah\'e4r\'e4ja, having already been trained in the }{
\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 k\'f1atriya}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  spirit, would not accept the brahminical philosophy. We have practical experience of this incompatibility of the brahminical and }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 k\'f1atriya}{
