\insrsid11288628 \emdash my dear son; }{\cs337\i\insrsid11288628 gaccha}{\insrsid11288628 \emdash go; }{\cs337\i\insrsid11288628 bhadram}{\insrsid11288628 \emdash good fortune; }{\cs337\i\insrsid11288628 te}{\insrsid11288628 \emdash for you; }{
\cs337\i\insrsid11288628 yamun\'e4y\'e4\'f9}{\insrsid11288628 \emdash of the Yamun\'e4; }{\cs337\i\insrsid11288628 ta\'f6am}{\insrsid11288628 \emdash bank; }{\cs337\i\insrsid11288628 \'e7uci}{\insrsid11288628 \emdash being purified; }{
\cs337\i\insrsid11288628 pu\'ebyam}{\insrsid11288628 \emdash the holy; }{\cs337\i\insrsid11288628 madhuvanam}{\insrsid11288628 \emdash of the name Madhuvana; }{\cs337\i\insrsid11288628 yatra}{\insrsid11288628 \emdash where; }{\cs337\i\insrsid11288628 s
\'e4nnidhyam}{\insrsid11288628 \emdash being nearer; }{\cs337\i\insrsid11288628 nityad\'e4}{\insrsid11288628 \emdash always; }{\cs337\i\insrsid11288628 hare\'f9}{\insrsid11288628 \emdash of the Supreme Personality of Godhead.
\par }\pard \s268\qc \li0\ri0\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11288628 {\b\cf11\insrsid11288628\charrsid3754542 TRANSLATION}{\insrsid11288628 
\par }\pard\plain \s280\qj \li0\ri0\sb360\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11288628 \b\f35\fs28\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v\insrsid11288628 TRANSLATION
\par }\pard\plain \s289\qj \fi360\li0\ri0\sb36\sa36\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11288628 \b\f35\fs24\cf13\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\insrsid11288628 
My dear boy, I therefore wish all good fortune for you. You should go to the bank of the Yamun\'e4, where there is a virtuous forest n
amed Madhuvana, and there be purified. Just by going there, one draws nearer to the Supreme Personality of Godhead, who always lives there.
\par }\pard \s289\qc \fi360\li0\ri0\sb36\sa36\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11288628 {\fs28\cf11\insrsid11288628\charrsid3754542 PURPORT
\par }\pard\plain \s280\qj \li0\ri0\sb360\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11288628 \b\f35\fs28\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v\insrsid11288628 PURPORT
\par }\pard\plain \s124\qj \fi360\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11288628 \f35\fs28\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cf1\insrsid11288628 Both N\'e4rada Muni and Sun\'e9ti, the mother of Dhruva Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja, advised Dhruva Mah\'e4r\'e4ja to worship the Supreme Personality of Godhead. Now, N\'e4rada Muni is especially giving him directions how this worship of the Supreme Person can very quickly fructify. He recommends that Dhruva Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja go to the bank of the Yamun\'e4, where there is a forest of the name Madhuvana, and begin his meditation and worship there.
\par }\pard\plain \s216\qj \fi357\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11288628 \f35\fs28\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cf1\insrsid11288628 
Places of pilgrimage yield a special advantage for a devotee in quickly advancing his spiritual life. Lord K\'e5\'f1\'eba lives everywhere, but still it is very easy to approach Him in holy places of pilgrimage beca
use these places are inhabited by great sages. Lord \'c7r\'e9 K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a says that He lives wherever His devotees are chanting the glories of His transcendental activities. There are many places of pilgrimage in India, and especially prominent are Badrin\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba, Dv\'e4rak\'e4, R\'e4me\'e7varam and Jagann\'e4tha Pur
\'e9. These sacred places are called the four }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 dh\'e4mas.}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 Dh\'e4ma}{\cf1\insrsid11288628 
 refers to a place where one can immediately contact the Supreme Lord. To go to Badrin\'e4r\'e4ya\'eba one has to pass through Hardv\'e4ra on the path to the Supreme Personality 
of Godhead. Similarly, there are other holy places of pilgrimage, such as Pray\'e4g (Allahabad) and Mathur\'e4, and the topmost of them all is V\'e5nd\'e4
vana. Unless one is very advanced in spiritual life, it is recommended that he live in such holy places and execute devotional service there. But an advanced devotee like N\'e4
rada Muni who is engaged in preaching work can serve the Supreme Lord anywhere. Sometimes he even goes to the hellish planets. Hellish conditions do not affect N\'e4rada Muni because he is engaged in 
greatly responsible activities in devotional service. According to the statement of N\'e4rada Muni, Madhuvana, which is still existing in the V\'e5nd\'e4vana area, in the district of Mathur\'e4
, is a most sacred place. Many saintly persons still live there and engage in the devotional service of the Lord.
\par There are twelve forests in the area of V\'e5nd\'e4vana, and Madhuvana is one of them. Pilgrims from all parts of India assemble together and visit all twelve of these forests. There are five forests on the eastern bank of the Yamun\'e4
: Bhadravana, Bilvavana, Lauhavana, Bh\'e4\'eb\'f2\'e9ravana and Mah\'e4vana. On the western side of the bank there are seven: Madhuvana, T\'e4lavana, Kumudavana, Bahul\'e4vana, K\'e4myavana, Khadiravana and V\'e5nd\'e4
vana. In those twelve forests there are different }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 gh\'e4\'f6s,}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  or bathing places. They are listed as follows: (1) Avimukta, (2) Adhir\'fc\'f2ha, (3) Guhyat\'e9rtha, (4) Pray\'e4gat\'e9
rtha, (5) Kanakhala, (6) Tindukat\'e9rtha, (7) S\'fcryat\'e9rtha, (8) Va\'f6asv\'e4m\'e9, (9) Dhruvagh\'e4\'f6a (Dhruvagh\'e4\'f6a, where there are many nice  fruit and flower trees, is famous because Dhruva Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja meditated and underwent severe penances and austerities there in an elevated spot), (10) \'c5\'f1it\'e9rtha, (11) Mok\'f1at\'e9rtha, (12) Budhat\'e9rtha, (13) Gokar\'eba, (14) K\'e5\'f1\'ebaga\'ecg\'e4, (15) Vaiku\'eb\'f6ha, (16) Asiku\'eb\'f2
a, (17) Catu\'f9s\'e4mudrika-k\'fcpa, (18) Akr\'fcrat\'e9rtha (when K\'e5\'f1\'eba and Balar\'e4ma were going to Mathur\'e4 in the chariot driven by Akr\'fcra, all of them took baths in this }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 gh\'e4\'f6}{\cf1\insrsid11288628 
), (19) Y\'e4j\'efigviprasth\'e4na, (20) Kubja-k\'fcpa, (21) Ra\'ecgasth\'e4la, (22) Ma\'efchasth\'e4la, (23) Ma\'ecllayuddhasth\'e4na, and (24) Da\'e7\'e4\'e7vamedha.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s304\qr \li0\ri0\sb300\sa120\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11288628 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid11288628 SB 4.8.43
