ja had already been instructed how to practice the eightfold }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 yoga}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  system, which is known as }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 a\'f1\'f6\'e4\'ecga-yoga.}{\cf1\insrsid11288628 
 This system is explained in our }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4 As It Is,}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  in the chapter entitled, }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 "S\'e4\'eckhya-yoga,}{\cf1\insrsid11288628 
" pages 319-322. It is understood that in }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 a\'f1\'f6\'e4\'ecga-yoga}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  one practices settling the mind and then concentrating it on the form of Lord Vi\'f1\'eb
u, as will be described in the following verses. It is clearly stated here that }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 a\'f1\'f6\'e4\'ecga-yoga}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  is not a bodily gymnastic exercise, but a practice to concentrate the mind on the form of Vi\'f1
\'ebu. Before sitting on his }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 \'e4sana,}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  which is also described in }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4,}{\cf1\insrsid11288628 
 one has to cleanse himself very nicely in clear or sacred water thrice daily. The water of the Yamun\'e4 is naturally very clear and pure, and thus if anyone bathes there three times, undoubtedly he will be very greatly purified externally. N\'e4
rada Muni, therefore, instructed Dhruva Mah\'e4r\'e4ja to go to the bank of the Yamun\'e4 and thus become externally purified. This is part of the gradual process of practicing mystic }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 yoga.
\par 
