This verse clearly explains how one has to meditate on the form of the Lord. Impersonal meditation is a bogus invention of modern days. In none of the Vedic literatures is impersonal meditation recommended. In }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 Bhagavad-g\'e9
t\'e4,}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  when meditation is recommended, the word "}{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 mat-para\'f9,"}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  which means "pertaining to Me," is used. Any Vi\'f1\'ebu form pertains to Lord K\'e5\'f1\'eba because Lord K\'e5
\'f1\'eba is the original Vi\'f1\'ebu form. Sometimes someone tries to meditate upon the impersonal Brahman, which is described in }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  as }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 avyakta,}{
\cf1\insrsid11288628  meaning unmanifested or impersonal. But it is remarked by the Lord Himself that those who are attached to this impersonal feature of the Lord suffer a very troublesome task because no o
ne can concentrate on the impersonal feature. One has to concentrate on the form of the Lord, which is described here in connection with Dhruva Mah\'e4r\'e4ja's meditation. As will be apparent from later descriptions, Dhruva Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja perfected this kind of meditation, and his }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 yoga}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  was successful.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s304\qr \li0\ri0\sb300\sa120\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11288628 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid11288628 SB 4.8.46
