rada Muni does not say that. Rather, he gives the description of the Lord from authoritative sources. He is himself an authority, and he is able to go to Vaiku\'eb\'f6
haloka and see the Lord personally; therefore his description of the bodily features of the Lord is not imagination. Sometimes we give instructions t
o our students about the bodily features of the Lord, and they paint Him. Their paintings are not imaginary. The description is given through disciplic succession, just like that given by N\'e4
rada Muni, who sees the Lord and describes His bodily features. Therefore, such descriptions should be accepted, and if they are painted, that is not imaginative painting.
