This important verse has been discussed by many stalwart commentators. Why was Dhruva Mah\'e4r\'e4ja not very pleased,
 even after achieving the goal of life he desired? A pure devotee is always free from any kind of material desires. In the material world, one's material desires are all most demonic; one thinks of others as one's enemies, one thinks of revenge against on
e's enemies, one aspires to become the topmost leader or topmost person in this material world, and th}{\cf1\insrsid4325450 us one competes with all others, etc.}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  This has been described in the }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 
Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4,}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  Sixteenth Chapter, as asuric. A pure devotee has no demand from the Lor
d. His only concern is to serve the Lord sincerely and seriously, and he is not at all concerned about what will happen in the future. In the }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 Mukunda-m\'e4l\'e4-stotra,}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  King Kula\'e7
ekhara, author of the book, states in his prayer: "My dear Lord, I don't want any position of sense gratification within this material world. I simply want to engage in Your service perpetually." Similarly, Lord Caitanya, in His }{
\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 \'c7ik\'f1\'e4\'f1\'f6aka,}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  also prayed, "My Lord, I do not want any amount of material wealth, I do not want any number of ma
terialistic followers, nor do I want any attractive wife to enjoy. The only thing I want is that I may engage life after life in Your service." Lord Caitanya did not pray even for }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 mukti,}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  or liberation.

