is material world. Sometimes he gets the body of a demigod, and he thinks that body to be his identity. Similarly, when he gets the body of a dog he identifies his self with that body. But for the Supreme Personality of Godhead there is no such distinctio
n between the body and the soul. }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4,}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  therefore, certifies that anyone who thinks of K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a as an ordinary human being is without knowledge of His transcendental nature and is a great fool. The Lord says, }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 na m\'e4\'e0 karm\'e4\'ebi limpanti:}{\cf1\insrsid13773841  }{\cf1\insrsid11288628 He is n}{
\cf1\insrsid6504715 ot affected by anything He does}{\cf1\insrsid11288628 
 because He is never contaminated by the material modes of nature. That we have a material body proves that we are infected by the three material modes of nature. The Lord says to Arjuna, "You and I had many, many births previously, but
 I remember everything, whereas you do not." That is the difference between the living entity, or conditioned soul, and the Supreme Soul. The Supersoul, the Supreme Personality of Godhead, has no material body, and because He has no material body, He is n
ot affected by any work He executes. There are many M\'e4y\'e4v\'e4d\'e9 philosophers who consider that K\'e5\'f1\'eba's body is the effect of a concentration of the material mode of goodness, and they distinguish K\'e5\'f1\'eba's soul from K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a's body. The real situation, however, is that the body of the conditioned soul, even if he has a large accumulation of material goodness, is material, whereas K\'e5\'f1\'eba's body is never material; it is transcendental. K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a has no false ego, for He does not identify Himself with the false and temporary body. His body }{\cf1\insrsid6504715 is always eternal; He descends }{\cf1\insrsid11288628 o}{\cf1\insrsid6504715 n this world in His own original}{\cf1\insrsid11288628 
 spiritual body. This is explained in }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  as }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 para\'e0 bh\'e4vam.}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  The words }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 para\'e0 bh\'e4va\'e0}{
\cf1\insrsid11288628  }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid6504715 divyam }{\cf1\insrsid11288628 are especially significant in understanding K\'e5\'f1\'eba's personality.
