\par }\pard\plain \s124\qj \fi360\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11288628 \f35\fs28\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cf1\insrsid11288628 Dhruva Mah\'e4r\'e4ja was a liber
ated soul, and actually he was not angry with anyone. But because he was the ruler, it was his duty to become angry for some time in order to keep law and order in the state. His brother, Uttama, was without fault, yet he was killed by one of the Yak\'f1
as. It was the duty of Dhruva Mah\'e4r\'e4ja to kill the offender (life for life) because Dhruva was the king. When the challenge came, Dhruva Mah\'e4r\'e4ja fought vehemently and punished the Yak\'f1
as sufficiently. But anger is such that if one increases it, it increases unlimitedly. In order that Dhruva Mah\'e4r\'e4ja's kingly anger not exceed the limit, Manu was kind enough to check his grandson. Dhruva Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja could understand the purpose of his grandfather, and he immediately stopped the fighting. The words }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 \'e7rutena bh\'fcyas\'e4,}{\cf1\insrsid10497941  by constantly hearing,}{\cf1\insrsid11288628 
 are very important in this verse. By constantly hearing about devotional service, one can check the force of anger, which is detrimental to the process of devotional service. \'c7r\'e9la Par\'e9k\'f1it Mah\'e4r\'e4ja said that the constant hearing of t
he pastimes of the Lord is the panacea for all material diseases. Everyone, therefore, should hear about the Supreme Personality of Godhead constantly. By hearing one can always remain in equilibrium, and thus his progress in spiritual life will not be ha
mpered.
\par }\pard\plain \s216\qj \fi357\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11288628 \f35\fs28\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cf1\insrsid11288628 Dhruva Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja's becoming angry with the miscreants was quite appropriate. There is a short story in this connection about a snake who became a devotee upon instruction by N\'e4rada, who instructed him not to bite anymore. Since ordinarily a snake's b
usiness is to fatally bite other living entities, as a devotee he was forbidden to do so. Unfortunately, people took advantage of this nonviolence on the part of the snake, especially the children, who began to throw stones at him. He did not bite anyone,
 however, because it was the instruction of his spiritual master. After a while, when the snake met his spiritual master, N\'e4
rada, he complained, "I have given up the bad habit of biting innocent living entities, but they are mistreating me by throwing stones at me." Upon hearing this, N\'e4
rada Muni instructed him, "Don't bite, but do not forget to expand your hood as if you were going to bite. Then they will go away." Similarly, a devotee is always nonviolent; he is qualified with all good characteristics. But
, in the common world, when there is mischief made by others, he should not forget to become angry, at least for the time being, in order to drive away the miscreants.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s304\qj \li0\ri0\sb300\sa120\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11288628 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid11288628 SB 4.11.32
