, was very pleased with Dhruva Mah\'e4r\'e4ja because he did not ask him for anything materially enjoyable. Kuvera is one of the demigods, so one may put forward the argument, }{\cf1\insrsid92854 "Why did Dhruva Mah\'e4r\'e4ja take }{\cf1\insrsid11288628 
benediction from a demigod?" The answer is that for a Vai\'f1\'ebava there is no objection }{\cf1\insrsid6511997 in taking }{\cf1\insrsid11288628 benediction from a demigod if it is favorable for advancing K\'e5\'f1\'eba consciousness. The }{
\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 gop\'e9s,}{\cf1\insrsid11288628  for example, worshiped K\'e4ty\'e4yan\'e9, a demigoddess, but the only benediction they wanted from the goddess was to have K\'e5\'f1\'eba as their husband. A Vai\'f1\'eb
ava is not interested in asking any benediction from the demigods, nor is he interested in asking benedictions from the Supreme Personality of Godhead. It is said in the }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid11288628 Bh\'e4gavatam}{\cf1\insrsid11288628 
 that liberation can be offered by the Supreme Person, but even if a pure devotee is offered liberation by the Supreme Lord, he refuses to accept it. Dhruva Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja did not ask Kuvera for transference to the spiritual world, which is called liberation; he simply asked that wherever he would remain\emdash whether in the spiritual or material world\emdash 
he would always remember the Supreme Personality of Godhead. A Vai\'f1\'ebava is always respectful to everyone. So when Kuvera offered him a benediction, he did not refuse it. But he wanted something which would be favorable to his advancement in K\'e5
\'f1\'eba consciousness.
