 The change of body is enacted by the reactions of the three modes of nature. One who has understood the fixed position 
of the soul should not be disturbed by the incoming and outgoing interactions of the modes of material nature in the form of happiness and distress. In }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{\cs380\cf1\insrsid11227510  also}{
\cs380\cf1\insrsid5197105\charrsid5197105  Lord K\'e5\'f1\'eba recommends that since happiness and distress come and go due to the intera
ction of the modes of nature on the body, one should not be disturbed by such external movements. Even though one is sometimes absorbed in such external movements, he has to learn to tolerate them. The living entity should be always indifferent to the act
ion and reaction of the external body.
\par }\pard\plain \s216\qj \fi357\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs28\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs383\cf1\insrsid5197105\charrsid5197105 Lord K\'e5\'f1\'eba says in }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{
