 is not used, but the word used then is }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 r\'e4ya\'f9,}{\cf1\insrsid15277743  which means wealth.}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  In India there are still wealthy persons who are recognized by the state as }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 r
\'e4ya.}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  A great devotee of Lord Caitanya Mah\'e4prabhu was called R\'e4m\'e4nanda R\'e4ya because he was governor of Madras and very rich. There are still many holders of the title }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 r\'e4ya}{
\insrsid15277743 \emdash }{\cf1\insrsid5197105 R\'e4ya B}{\cf1\insrsid15277743 \'e4}{\cf1\insrsid5197105 h}{\cf1\insrsid15277743 \'e4}{\cf1\insrsid5197105 dur, R\'e4ya Chaudhuri and so on. The }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 d\'e4r\'e4\'f9,}{
\cf1\insrsid5197105  or wife, is not permitted to be offered to the }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 br\'e4hma\'ebas.}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  Everything is offered to worthy persons who are able to accept charity, but nowhere is it found that one offers his 
wife; therefore in this case the reading }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 r\'e4ya\'f9}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  is more accurate than }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 d\'e4r\'e4\'f9.}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  Also, since P\'e5thu Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja offered everything to the Kum\'e4ras, the word }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 ko\'e7a\'f9}{\cf1\insrsid15277743  (treasury}{\cf1\insrsid5197105 ) need not be separately mentioned. Kings and emperors used to keep a private treasury which was known as }{
\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 ratna-bh\'e4\'eb\'f2a.}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  The }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 ratna-bh\'e4\'eb\'f2a}{\cf1\insrsid5197105 
 was a special treasury room which contained special jewelries, such as bangles, necklaces and so on, which were presented to the king by the citizens. This jewelry was kept separate from the regular treasury house
 where all the collected revenues were kept. Thus P\'e5thu Mah\'e4r\'e4ja offered his stock of private jewelry to the lotus feet of the Kum\'e4ras. It has already been admitted that all the King's property belonged to the }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 br
\'e4hma\'ebas}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  and that P\'e5thu Mah\'e4r\'e4ja was simply using it for the welfare of the state. If it were actually the property of the }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 br\'e4hma\'ebas,}{\cf1\insrsid5197105 
 how could it be offered again to them? In this regard, \'c7r\'e9p\'e4da \'c7r\'e9dhara Sv\'e4m\'e9 has explained that this offering is just like the servant's offering of food to his maste
r. The food already belongs to the master, for the master has purchased it, but the servant, by preparing food, makes it acceptable to the master and thus offers it to him. In this way, everything belonging to P\'e5thu Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja was offered to the Kum\'e4ras.
\par }{\cf1\insrsid14643438 
