\cf1\insrsid5197105  incarnations of the Supreme Personality of Godhead, and as such he appeared on the surface of the earth to execute the orders of the Supreme. As stated in }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4,}{\cf1\insrsid5197105 
 the Supreme Lord is the proprietor of all planets, and He is always anxious to see that in 
each and every planet the living entities are happily living and executing their duties. As soon as there is some discrepancy in the execution of duties, the Lord appears on earth, as confirmed in }{\cs347\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{
\cf1\insrsid5197105 : }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 yad\'e4 yad\'e4 hi dharmasya gl\'e4nir bhavati bh\'e4rata}{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid10975489  }{\cf1\insrsid10975489 (Bg. 4.7)}{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 .
\par }\pard\plain \s216\qj \fi357\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs28\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs383\cf1\insrsid5197105\charrsid5197105 
Since there were so many discrepancies during the reign of King Vena, the Lord sent His most confidential devotee}{\cs383\cf1\insrsid10975489 ,}{\cs383\cf1\insrsid5197105\charrsid5197105  Mah\'e4r\'e4ja P\'e5
thu to settle things. Therefore, after executing the orders of the Supreme Personality of Godhead and settling the affairs of the world, Mah\'e4r\'e4ja P\'e5
thu was ready to retire. He had been exemplary in his governmental administration, and now he was to become exemplary in his retirement. He divided all his property amongst his sons and appointed them to rule the world, and t
hen he went to the forest with his wife. It is significant in this connection that it is said that Mah\'e4r\'e4ja P\'e5
thu retired alone and at the same time took his wife with him. According to Vedic principles, when retiring from family life, one can take his wif
e with him, for the husband and wife are considered to be one unit. Thus they can both combinedly perform austerities for liberation. This is the path that Mah\'e4r\'e4ja P\'e5
thu, who was an exemplary character, followed, and this is also the way of Vedic civiliza
tion. One should not simply remain at home until the time of death, but should separate from family life at a timely moment and prepare himself to go back to Godhead. As a }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 \'e7akty\'e4ve\'e7a}{
\cs383\cf1\insrsid5197105\charrsid5197105  incarnation of God who had actually come from Vaiku\'eb\'f6ha as a representative of K\'e5\'f1\'eba, Mah\'e4r\'e4ja P\'e5
thu was certain to go back to Godhead. Nonetheless in order to set the example in all ways, he also underwent severe austerities in the }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 tapo-vana.}{\cs383\cf1\insrsid5197105\charrsid5197105 
 It appears that in those days there were many }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 tapo-vanas,}{\cs383\cf1\insrsid5197105\charrsid5197105  or forests especially meant
 for retirement and the practice of austerities. Indeed, it was compulsory for everyone to go to the }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 tapo-vana}{\cs383\cf1\insrsid5197105\charrsid5197105 
 to fully accept the shelter of the Supreme Personality of Godhead, for it is very difficult to retire from family life and at the same time remain at home.}{\cs383\cf1\insrsid5197105 
\par }{\cs383\cf1\insrsid14643438\charrsid5197105 
\par }\pard\plain \s304\qr \li0\ri0\sb300\sa120\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid5197105 SB 4.23.4
