According to Vedic scriptures, a woman who dies with her husband, or enters into the fire in which her husband is burning, also enters the same planet her husband attains. In this materi
al world there is a planet known as Patiloka, just as there is a planet known as Pit\'e5loka. But in this verse the word }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 pati-loka}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  does not refer to any planet within this material universe, for P\'e5
thu Mah\'e4r\'e4ja, being topmost amongst self-realized souls, certainly returned home, back to Godhead, and attained one of the Vaiku\'eb\'f6ha pl}{\cf1\insrsid532114 anets. Queen Arci also entered }{\i\cf1\insrsid532114\charrsid532114 p}{
\i\cf1\insrsid5197105\charrsid532114 atiloka}{\cf1\insrsid5197105 , but this planet is not in the material universe, for she actually entered the planet which her husband attained. In the material world also, w
hen a woman dies with her husband, she again unites with him in the next birth. Similarly, Mah\'e4r\'e4ja P\'e5thu and Queen Arci united in the Vaiku\'eb\'f6ha planets. In the Vaiku\'eb\'f6
ha planets there are husbands and wives, but there is no question of their giving birth to children }{\cf1\insrsid532114 n}{\cf1\insrsid5197105 or having sex life. In the Vaiku\'eb\'f6
ha planets both husbands and wives are extraordinarily beautiful, and they are attracted to one another, but they do not enjoy sex life. Indeed, they consider sex not to be very relishable because both husband and wife are always absorbed in K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a consciousness and in glorifying and chanting the glories of the Lord.
\par }\pard\plain \s216\qj \fi357\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs28\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs383\cf1\insrsid5197105\charrsid5197105 According to Bhaktivinoda \'d6h\'e4
