 means "near the sea." The reservoir of water was like a bay, for it was not very far from the sea. The word }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 upa,}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  meaning "more or less," is used in many ways, as in the word }{
\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 upapati,}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  which indicates a husband "more or less," that is to say, a lover who is acting like a husband. }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 Upa}{\cf1\insrsid13066956  also means greater, smaller or nearer.}{
\cf1\insrsid5197105  Considering all these points, the reservoir of water which was seen by the Pracet\'e4
s while they were traveling was actually a large bay or lake. And unlike the sea or ocean, which has turbulent waves, this reservoir was very calm and quiet. Indeed, the water was so clear that it seemed like the mind of some gr
eat soul. There may be many great souls\emdash }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 j\'ef\'e4n\'e9s, yog\'e9s}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  and }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 bhaktas,}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  or pure devotees, are also called great souls\emdash 
but they are very rarely found. One can find many great souls amongst }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 yog\'e9s}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  and }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 j\'ef\'e4n\'e9s,}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  but a truly great soul, a pure devotee of}{
\cf1\insrsid13066956  the Lord}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  who is fully surrendered to the Lord, is very rarely found (}{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 sa mah\'e4tm\'e4 sudurlabha\'f9,}{\cf1\insrsid5197105 
 Bg. 7.19). A devotee's mind is always calm, quiet and desireless because he is always }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 any\'e4bhil\'e4\'f1it\'e4-\'e7\'fcnyam,}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  }{\cf1\insrsid13066956 and he has no desire }{\cf1\insrsid5197105 
other than to serve K\'e5\'f1\'eba as His personal servant, friend, father, mother or conjugal lover. Due to his association with K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a, a devotee is always very calm and cool. It is also significant that within that reservoir all the aquatics were also very calm and quiet. Because the disciples of a devotee have taken shelter of a great }{\cf1\insrsid13066956 man}{\cf1\insrsid5197105 
, they become very calm and quiet and are not agitated by the waves of the material world.
\par }\pard\plain \s216\qj \fi357\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs28\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cf1\insrsid5197105 
This material world is often described as an ocean of nescience. In such an ocean, everything is agitated. The mind of a great devotee is also like an ocean or a very large lake, but there is no agitation. As stated in }{\cs347\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 
Bhagavad-g\'e9t}{\cs347\i\cf1\insrsid13066956 \'e4}{\cf1\insrsid5197105 : }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 vyavas\'e4y\'e4tmik\'e4 buddhir ekeha kuru-nandana}{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid13066956  }{\cs347\cf1\insrsid13066956\charrsid13066956 (Bg.}{
\cs347\i\cf1\insrsid13066956  }{\cf1\insrsid13066956 2.41)}{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 .}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  Those who are fixed in the service of the Lord are not agitated by anything. It is also stated in }{\cs347\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 Bhagavad-g\'e9t
}{\cs347\i\cf1\insrsid13066956 \'e4}{\cf1\insrsid5197105 : }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 yasmin sthito na du\'f9khena guru\'eb\'e4pi vic\'e4lyate}{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid13066956  }{\cs347\cf1\insrsid13066956\charrsid13066956 (Bg.}{
\cs347\i\cf1\insrsid13066956  }{\cf1\insrsid13066956 6.22)}{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 .}{\cf1\insrsid5197105 
 Even if he suffers some reversals in life, a devotee is never agitated. Therefore whoever takes shelter of a great soul or a great devotee becomes pacified. In the }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 Caitanya-carit\'e4m\'e5ta}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  it i
s stated: }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 k\'e5\'f1\'eba-bhakta}{\insrsid11475879 \emdash }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 ni\'f1k\'e4ma, ataeva '\'e7\'e4nta}{\cf1\insrsid5197105 '}{\cf1\insrsid11475879  (Cc. }{\cs347\i\cf1\insrsid11475879 Madhya}{
\cf1\insrsid11475879  19.149). }{\cf1\insrsid5197105  A devotee of Lord K\'e5\'f1\'eba is always peaceful because he has no desire, whereas the }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 yog\'e9s, karm\'e9s}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  and }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 j\'ef
\'e4n\'e9s}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  have so many desires to fulfill. One may argue that the devotees have desires, fo
r they wish to go home, back to Godhead, but such a desire does not agitate the mind. Although he wishes to go back to Godhead, a devotee is satisfied in any }{\cf1\insrsid11475879 condition of life. Consequently}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  the word }{
\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 mahan-mana\'f9}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  is used in this verse to indicate that the reservoir of water was as calm and quiet as the mind of a great devotee.
\par }{\cf1\insrsid14643438 
