\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 vede\'f1u durlabham adurlabham \'e4tma-bhaktau.}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  This indicates that it is very difficult for one to attain the ultimate goal of life and reach the supreme destination, Vaiku\'eb\'f6haloka or Goloka V\'e5nd
\'e4vana, simply by studying Ved\'e4nta philosophy or Vedic literature. However, this highest perfectional stage can be attained by the devotees very easily. That is the meaning of }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 vede\'f1u durlabham adurlabham \'e4
tma-bhaktau.}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  The same point is confirmed by Lord \'c7iva in this verse. The Lord is very difficult for the }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 karma-yog\'e9s, j\'ef\'e4na-yog\'e9s}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  and }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 dhy
\'e4na-yog\'e9s}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  to attain. Those who are }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 bhakti-yog\'e9s,}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  however, have no difficulty at all. In the word }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 sv\'e4r\'e4jyasya, svar}{\cf1\insrsid5197105 
 refers to Svargaloka, the heavenly planet, and }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 sv\'e4r\'e4jya}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  refers to the ruler of the heavenly planet, Indra. Generally, }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 karm\'e9s}{\cf1\insrsid5197105 
 desire elevation to heavenly planets, but King Indra desires to become perfect in }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 bhakti-yoga.}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  Those who identify themselves as }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 aha\'e0 brahm\'e4smi}{\cf1\insrsid5197105 
 ("I am the Supreme Brahman, one with the Absolute Truth") also ultimately desire to attain perfect liberation in the Vaiku\'eb\'f6ha planets or Goloka V\'e5nd\'e4vana. In }{\cs347\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 Bhagavad-}{
\cs347\i\cf1\insrsid8601574\charrsid8601574 g\'e9t\'e4}{\cs347\cf1\insrsid14184656\charrsid14184656  }{\cf1\insrsid5197105 it is said:
