al world there is a great illusion which covers real intelligence. A man in the mode of passion wants to work very hard to derive some benefit, but he does not know that time will never allow him to enjoy a}{\cf1\insrsid12530471 
nything permanently. Compared to}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  the work one expends, the gain is not so profitable. Even if it is profitable, it is not without its distresses. If a man is not born rich}{\cf1\insrsid12530471 ,}{\cf1\insrsid5197105 
 and he wants to purchase a house, cars and other material things, he has to work hard day and night for many years in order to possess them. Thus happiness is not attained without undergoing some distress.
\par }\pard\plain \s216\qj \fi357\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs28\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cf1\insrsid8865366 Actually}{\cf1\insrsid5197105 
 pure happiness cannot be had within this material world. If we wish to enjoy something, we must suffer for something else. On the whole, suffering is the nature of this mater
ial world, and whatever enjoyment we are trying to achieve is simply illusion. After all, we have to suffer the miseries of birth, old age, disease and death. We may discover many fine medicines, but it is not possible to stop the sufferings of }{
\cf1\insrsid8865366 disease or death. Actually}{\cf1\insrsid5197105 
 medicine is not the counteracting agent for either disease or death. On the whole there is no happiness in this material world, but an illusioned person works very hard for so-called happiness. Indeed, this process of working hard is actual
ly taken for happiness. This is called illusion.
\par Therefore N\'e4rada Muni asked King Pr\'e4c\'e9nabarhi\'f1at what he desired to attain by performing so many costly sacrifices. Even if one attains a heavenly planet, he cannot avoid the distresses of birth, old age, di
sease and death. Someone may argue that even devotees have to undergo many distresses in executing austerities and penances connected wit}{\cf1\insrsid8865366 h devotional service. Of course}{\cf1\insrsid5197105 
 for the neophytes the routine of devotional service may be very painful, but at least they have the hope that they will ultimately be able to avoid all kinds of distresses and achieve the highest perfectional stage of happiness. For the common }{
\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 karm\'e9s,}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  there is no such hope because even if they are promoted to the higher planetary systems, they are not guaranteed freedom from the miseries of birth, old age, disease and death. Even Lord Brahm
\'e4, who is situated in the highest planetary system (Brahmaloka), has to die. Lord Brahm\'e4's birth and death may be different from an ordinary man's, but wit
hin this material world he cannot avoid the distresses of birth, old age, disease and death. If one is at all serious about attaining liberation from these miseries, he must take to devotional service. This is confirmed by the Lord Himself in }{
\cs347\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 Bhagavad-}{\cs347\i\cf1\insrsid8601574\charrsid8601574 g\'e9t\'e4}{\cf1\insrsid5197105 :
