\par }\pard\plain \s124\qj \fi360\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs28\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cf1\insrsid5197105 
Liberation means becoming free from the relativities of the world. Unless one is self-realized, he has to undergo the dual struggle of the relative world. In }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 Bhagavad-}{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid8601574\charrsid8601574 g\'e9t\'e4}{
\cf1\insrsid5197105  Lord K\'e5\'f1\'eba advises Arjuna to conquer all relativities through tolerance. Lord K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a points out that it is the relativities like winter and summer that give us trouble in the material world. In the winter we do not like taking a bath, but in the summer we wish to take a bath twice, thrice or more a day. Thus K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a advises us not to be disturbed by such relativities and dualities when they come and go.
\par }\pard\plain \s216\qj \fi357\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs28\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cf1\insrsid5197105 
The common man has to undergo much austerity to become equipoised before dualities. One who becomes agitated by the relativities of life has accepted a relative position and must therefore undergo the austerities prescribed in the }{
\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 \'e7\'e4stras}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  to transcend the material body and put an end to material existence. King Malayadhvaja underwent severe austerities by leaving his home, going to Kul\'e4
cala, taking his bath in the sacred rivers and eating only vegetables like stems, roots, seeds, flowers and leaves, avoiding any cooked food or grains. These are very, very austere practices. In this age it is very difficult to leave home 
and go to the forest or the Himalayas to adopt the processes of austerity. Indeed, it is almost impossible. If one is even advised to give up meat-eating, drinking, gambling and illicit sex, one will fail to do so. What, then, would a person do if he went
 to the Himalayas or Kul\'e4cala? Such acts of renunciation are not possible in this age; therefore Lord K\'e5\'f1\'eba has advised us to accept the }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 bhakti-yoga}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  process. }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 
Bhakti-yoga}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  will automatically liberate a person from the dualities of life. In }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 bhakti-yoga,}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  K\'e5\'f1\'eba is the center, and K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a is always transcendental. Thus in order to transcend dualities, one must always engage in the service of the Lord, as confirmed by }{\cs347\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 Bhagavad-}{\cs347\i\cf1\insrsid8601574\charrsid8601574 g\'e9t\'e4}{\cf1\insrsid5197105 :

\par }\pard\plain \s302\qc \li0\ri0\sb180\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid5197105 m\'e4\'e0 ca yo 'vyabhic\'e4re\'eba\line bhakti-yogena sevate\line sa gu\'eb\'e4n samat
\'e9tyait\'e4n\line brahma-bh\'fcy\'e4ya kalpate
\par }\pard\plain \s31\qj \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs28\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cf1\insrsid5197105 
"One who engages in full devotional service, who does not fall down in any circumstance, at once transcends the modes of material nature and thus comes to the level of Brahman."}{\cf1\insrsid4326734  }{\cs347\cf1\insrsid4326734\charrsid14184656 (Bg. }{
\cf1\insrsid4326734 14.26)}{\cf1\insrsid5197105 
\par }\pard\plain \s216\qj \fi357\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs28\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cf1\insrsid5197105 If one is factually engaged in the service of the Lord, }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 bhakti-yoga,}{
\cf1\insrsid5197105  he will automatically control his senses, his tongue and so many other things. Once engaged in the }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 bhakti-yoga}{\cf1\insrsid5197105 
 process with all sincerity, one will have no chance of falling down. Even if one falls down, there is no loss. One's devotional activiti
es may be stunned or choked for the time being, but as soon as there is another chance, the practitioner begins from the point where he left off.
\par }{\cf1\insrsid2320539 
\par }\pard\plain \s304\qr \li0\ri0\sb300\sa120\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid5197105 SB 4.28.38
