\'eba, and this forgetfulness is especially enhanced in the }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 g\'e5hastha-\'e4\'e7rama.}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  In the }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 g\'e5hastha-\'e4\'e7rama}{\cf1\insrsid5197105 
 a young man accepts a young wife who is very beautiful in the beginni
ng, but in due course of time, after giving birth to many children and becoming older and older, she demands many things from the husband to maintain the entire family. At such a time the wife becomes detestable to the very man who accepted her in her you
nger days. One becomes attached to the }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 g\'e5hastha-\'e4\'e7rama}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  for two reasons only\emdash 
the wife cooks palatable dishes for the satisfaction of her husband's tongue, and she gives him sexual pleasure at night. A person attached to the }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 g\'e5hastha-\'e4\'e7rama}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  is always thinking
 of these two things\emdash 
palatable food and sex enjoyment. The talks of the wife, which are enjoyed as a family recreation, and the talks of the children both attract the living entity. He thus forgets that he has to die someday and has to prepare for the next
 life if he wants to be put into a congenial body.
\par }\pard\plain \s216\qj \fi357\li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs28\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cf1\insrsid5197105 The deer in the flower garden is an allegory used by the great sage N\'e4
rada to point out to the King that the King himself is similarly entrapped by such surroundings. Actually everyone is surrounded by suc
h a family life, which misleads one. The living entity thus forgets that he has to return home, back to Godhead. He simply becomes entangled in family life. Prahl\'e4da Mah\'e4r\'e4ja has therefore hinted: }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 hitv\'e4tma-p\'e4ta
\'e0 g\'e5ham andha-k\'fcpa\'e0 vana\'e0 gato yad dharim \'e4\'e7rayeta.}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  Family life is considered a blind well (}{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 andha-k\'fcpam}{\cf1\insrsid5197105 
) into which a person falls and dies without help. Prahl\'e4da Mah\'e4r\'e4ja recommends that while one's senses are there and one is strong enough, he should abandon the }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 g\'e5hastha-\'e4\'e7rama}{\cf1\insrsid5197105 
 and take shelter of the lotus feet of the Lord, going to the forest of V\'e5nd\'e4vana. According to Vedic civilization, one has to give up family life at a certain age (the age of fifty), take }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 v\'e4naprastha}{
\cf1\insrsid5197105  and eventually remain alone as a }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 sanny\'e4s\'e9.}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  That is the prescribed method of Vedic civilization known as }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 var\'eb\'e4\'e7rama-dharma.}{
\cf1\insrsid5197105  When one takes }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 sanny\'e4sa}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  after enjoying family life, he pleases the Supreme Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu.
\par One has to understand one's position in family or worldly life. That is called intelligence. One should not remain always t
rapped in family life to satisfy his tongue and genitals in association with a wife. In such a way, one simply spoils his life. According to Vedic civilization, it is imperative to give up the family at a certain stage, by force if necessary. Unfortunatel
y, so-called followers of Vedic life do not give up their family even at the end of life, unless they are forced by death. There should be a thorough overhauling of the social system, and society should revert to the Vedic principles, that is, the four }{
\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 var\'ebas}{\cf1\insrsid5197105  and the four }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid5197105 \'e4\'e7ramas.
\par }{\cs337\i\cf1\insrsid929492 
